window.CV_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Images as Tensors","f":"p1-images-as-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An image array has shape (300, 451, 3). Which call turns it into the layout a PyTorch convolution expects?","o":["t.reshape(3, 300, 451)","t.permute(2, 0, 1)","t.view(3, 300, 451)","t.permute(2, 1, 0)"],"a":1,"w":"permute moves the channel axis to the front. reshape and view keep memory order and scramble the channels; permute(2, 1, 0) also swaps height and width."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Images as Tensors","f":"p1-images-as-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is torch.tensor([200], dtype=torch.uint8) + 100 ?","o":["300","255","44","An overflow error"],"a":2,"w":"uint8 arithmetic wraps modulo 256: 300 − 256 = 44. It does not saturate at 255 and it does not raise."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Images as Tensors","f":"p1-images-as-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which library returns pixels in BGR order by default?","o":["OpenCV","PIL","scikit-image","torchvision’s decode_image"],"a":0,"w":"cv2.imread gives BGR. The pixel (150, 225) of the cat came back as [124, 150, 190] from OpenCV and [190, 150, 124] from the others."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Images as Tensors","f":"p1-images-as-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On the Imagenette validation set, which mistake left ResNet-50’s accuracy almost unchanged?","o":["Feeding 0–255 values","Using reshape instead of permute","Normalising twice","Skipping normalisation (values 0 to 1)"],"a":3,"w":"Skipping normalisation cost about one point (87.18% to 86.22%). The other three scored 0.00%, 0.00% and 0.13%."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Images as Tensors","f":"p1-images-as-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After the standard ImageNet preprocessing, what range of values should a batch roughly have?","o":["0 to 255","About −2.1 to 2.6","0 to 1","−1 to 1 exactly"],"a":1,"w":"Black in the red channel is (0 − 0.485) / 0.229 = −2.118 and white in the blue channel is (1 − 0.406) / 0.225 = 2.64."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch & the Training Loop","f":"p2-pytorch-training-loop.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What should the very first training loss of a fresh 10-class classifier be, roughly?","o":["0","About 2.3","About 10","It can be anything"],"a":1,"w":"Near-uniform predictions give cross-entropy ln 10 = 2.303. The measured first loss was 2.41."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch & the Training Loop","f":"p2-pytorch-training-loop.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does forgetting opt.zero_grad() make training blow up?","o":["The optimiser stops updating","The loss is computed twice","The gradients become zero","backward() adds into .grad , so each step uses the sum of all previous gradients"],"a":3,"w":"Accumulation is by design. Without clearing, steps keep growing: the loss hit 120,332 by step 50 and then NaN."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch & the Training Loop","f":"p2-pytorch-training-loop.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does F.cross_entropy expect as its first argument?","o":["Raw logits","Softmax probabilities","Log-probabilities from log_softmax","One-hot labels"],"a":0,"w":"It applies log-softmax itself. Passing probabilities softmaxes twice; that run reached 49.7% instead of 52.6%."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch & the Training Loop","f":"p2-pytorch-training-loop.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An MLP trained with SGD at lr 0.2 ends with NaN loss. What will its test accuracy on balanced 10-class data be?","o":["About 50%","0%","About 10%","Impossible to say"],"a":2,"w":"NaN weights give the same prediction for every image, so it is right for exactly one class in ten: 10.00%."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch & the Training Loop","f":"p2-pytorch-training-loop.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why overfit one small batch before a long run?","o":["It initialises the weights better","A working model and loop must reach near-zero loss on it; if not, there is a bug, found in seconds","It measures test accuracy","It sets the learning rate automatically"],"a":1,"w":"The MLP reached 100% on 64 images by step 10. A model that cannot memorise 64 images has a bug in the model, the loss or the loop."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Colour Spaces, Gamma & Image Formats","f":"p3-colour-gamma-and-formats.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In sRGB, which stored value emits half the light of 255?","o":["128","64","About 188","About 220"],"a":2,"w":"sRGB is gamma-encoded. Linear 0.5 converts to 187.5; a stored 128 is only about 0.216 of full light."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Colour Spaces, Gamma & Image Formats","f":"p3-colour-gamma-and-formats.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which loader applies a JPEG’s EXIF orientation tag by default?","o":["cv2.imread","PIL.Image.open","torchvision.io.decode_jpeg","torchvision.io.decode_image"],"a":0,"w":"OpenCV rotated the 426 × 320 test file to 320 × 426. PIL needs ImageOps.exif_transpose , and torchvision needs apply_exif_orientation=True ."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Colour Spaces, Gamma & Image Formats","f":"p3-colour-gamma-and-formats.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Re-encoding Imagenette’s own JPEGs at quality 75 gave a higher PSNR than at quality 95. Why?","o":["q75 files are larger","PSNR is measured wrongly","q95 adds noise on purpose","The originals were saved at q75, so the same quantisation table re-produces nearly the same values"],"a":3,"w":"The originals averaged 25.4 KB, exactly the q75 size. Re-quantising with the same table changes almost nothing; q95’s different table moves every coefficient a little."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Colour Spaces, Gamma & Image Formats","f":"p3-colour-gamma-and-formats.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the greyscale value of RGB (200, 120, 40) with the standard luma weights?","o":["120","About 135","200","About 92"],"a":1,"w":"0.299 × 200 + 0.587 × 120 + 0.114 × 40 = 134.78. The plain mean is 120."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Colour Spaces, Gamma & Image Formats","f":"p3-colour-gamma-and-formats.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which change cost ResNet-50 the least accuracy on Imagenette?","o":["Rotating 90°","JPEG quality 8","Mirroring left to right","Swapping to BGR"],"a":2,"w":"Mirroring cost nothing (87.32% both ways): the model was trained with horizontal flips. Rotating cost 11 points, q8 12 points, BGR 4.9."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Geometry: Coordinates & Transforms","f":"p4-geometry-and-transforms.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is a point written as (x, y, 1) for geometric transforms?","o":["To store colour","So translation, like rotation and scale, becomes a matrix product","To make the maths use integers","To mark the pixel as valid"],"a":1,"w":"With homogeneous coordinates every affine transform, translation included, is a 3 × 3 matrix, so sequences of transforms compose into one matrix."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Geometry: Coordinates & Transforms","f":"p4-geometry-and-transforms.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You want to rotate an image about its centre (cx, cy). Which matrix, applied to (x, y, 1), does it?","o":["R · T(cx, cy)","T(cx, cy) · T(−cx, −cy) · R","T(−cx, −cy) · R · T(cx, cy)","T(cx, cy) · R · T(−cx, −cy)"],"a":3,"w":"Read right to left: move the centre to the origin, rotate, move back. It matched cv2.getRotationMatrix2D to 7 × 10⁻¹⁵."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Geometry: Coordinates & Transforms","f":"p4-geometry-and-transforms.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which interpolation should you use to resize a segmentation label mask?","o":["Nearest","Bilinear","Bicubic","Antialiased bilinear"],"a":0,"w":"Labels are categories. Blending class 1 and class 3 gives class 2, which is wrong. Nearest only ever copies existing labels."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Geometry: Coordinates & Transforms","f":"p4-geometry-and-transforms.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How many point correspondences determine a homography?","o":["2","3","4","9"],"a":2,"w":"H has 9 entries but is defined up to scale, so 8 unknowns; each point pair gives two equations. Four pairs (no three on a line) fix it. An affine transform needs only three."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Geometry: Coordinates & Transforms","f":"p4-geometry-and-transforms.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An image 451 pixels wide is flipped left to right. What does the box (100, 50, 220, 180) become?","o":["(351, 50, 231, 180)","(231, 50, 351, 180)","(100, 50, 220, 180)","(451, 50, 571, 180)"],"a":1,"w":"x maps to W − x, and the corners swap roles so that x1 < x2: (451 − 220, 50, 451 − 100, 180)."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Classical Filtering: Blur, Edges & Kernels","f":"p5-classical-filtering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A kernel’s weights sum to 0. What is its output on a perfectly flat region of the image?","o":["The original brightness","255","0","Half the brightness"],"a":2,"w":"Every pixel is the same value v, so the output is v × (sum of weights) = 0. Such kernels (Sobel, Laplacian) respond only to change."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Classical Filtering: Blur, Edges & Kernels","f":"p5-classical-filtering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which of these computes a true convolution, flipping the kernel?","o":["cv2.filter2D","scipy.ndimage.convolve","torch.nn.functional.conv2d","All of them"],"a":1,"w":"SciPy’s convolve flips the kernel; it gave exactly the negative of the hand-written Sobel result. OpenCV and PyTorch compute correlation."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Classical Filtering: Blur, Edges & Kernels","f":"p5-classical-filtering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How many multiplies per pixel does a separable 31 × 31 Gaussian need?","o":["961","31","480","62"],"a":3,"w":"Two 1-D passes of 31 weights each: 62, against 961 for the full 2-D kernel."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Classical Filtering: Blur, Edges & Kernels","f":"p5-classical-filtering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An image has 5% of its pixels randomly set to pure black or white. Which filter removed this best?","o":["A 3 × 3 median","A 5 × 5 box blur","A Gaussian blur, σ = 2","A bilateral filter"],"a":0,"w":"Median 3 × 3 reached 33.92 dB; the best averaging filter 28.66 and bilateral 21.37. A median ignores outliers; an average spreads them."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Classical Filtering: Blur, Edges & Kernels","f":"p5-classical-filtering.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did a 9 × 9 box blur with zero padding make the left edge of the photo dark?","o":["The kernel was not normalised","uint8 overflow","Part of the kernel averaged in zeros from outside the image","The Sobel step removed brightness"],"a":2,"w":"At the leftmost column, 4 of the kernel’s 9 columns lie outside the image, where zero padding supplies black: 68.7 instead of about 124."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Loading Image Data Fast","f":"p6-loading-image-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why must DataLoader code with workers sit under if __name__ == \"__main__\": on Windows?","o":["It makes the loader faster","It enables pinned memory","Workers are spawned as new processes that import the script, which would otherwise start loaders recursively","PyTorch requires it for CUDA"],"a":2,"w":"On Windows and macOS workers start by spawning, which re-imports the main module. The guard stops that import from re-running the training code."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Loading Image Data Fast","f":"p6-loading-image-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Decoding the same photo at 1,280 px instead of 320 px took how much longer?","o":["About the same","About 15 times","About 2 times","About 100 times"],"a":1,"w":"6.257 ms against 0.423 ms: decode cost grows roughly with the number of pixels (16 times as many here)."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Loading Image Data Fast","f":"p6-loading-image-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A loader produces 1,074 images/s and the GPU can train ResNet-18 on 2,857 images/s. Roughly how busy is the GPU?","o":["100%","About 85%","About 60%","About 38%"],"a":3,"w":"The pipeline runs at the slower stage: 1,074 / 2,857 ≈ 0.38. The GPU waits the rest of the time."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Loading Image Data Fast","f":"p6-loading-image-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which change raised 8-worker throughput from 3,939 to 6,357 images/s?","o":["Returning uint8 batches and normalising on the GPU","Pinning memory","Using batch size 256","Using PIL instead of torchvision"],"a":0,"w":"The workers skip the float conversion and normalisation, and each batch is a quarter of the bytes. Pinning made little difference here."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Loading Image Data Fast","f":"p6-loading-image-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How do you test whether training is limited by data loading?","o":["Double the learning rate","Check the loss curve","Compare the step rate on data already on the GPU with the real loop, and time waits on the loader","Count the model’s parameters"],"a":2,"w":"ResNet-18 ran 2,857 images/s on synthetic data and 2,813 with the real loader, waiting 3.9% of the time: compute-bound."}];
